{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1842 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8045e65a1d34d00d04a33f92325f9c3b3310b4a30535635bd736827e23cca3b1" :edb/db-before-t 1841 :edb/db-after-t 1842 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535154 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:09:50.149Z" 13194139535154 true] [17592186091952 1100 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=18-x^2$ over the interval $[1,4]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/c5f3d65642fe7a2944381ab2a888f0ae8a6f8e0defabf1ad545e6f3071f2b14b.png)" 13194139535154 true] [17592186091952 1100 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=18-x^2$ over the interval $[1,4]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/dd04ee54b1306d80622dc7f51fc24f94.png)" 13194139535154 false] [17592186091952 1145 "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)= 18 - x^2\n$$\n\nand let $\\mathcal A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(a)+ 2\\left(f(x_1) + f(x_2)+\\ldots + f(x_{n-1})\\right)+f(b) \\right]\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nis the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3$.\n- The corresponding interval is $[1,4]$, so we have $a=1$ and $b=4$.\n- The step size is given by\n$\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n} = \\dfrac{4 - 1}{3} = 1$.\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(1)+ 2\\left(f(2) + f(3)\\right) + f(4) \\right]\\cdot 1\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $17$ | $14$ | $9$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left[17 + 2\\left(14 + 9\\right)+ 2 \\right]\\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{65}{2}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[1,4]$, the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area." 13194139535154 false] [17592186091952 1145 "Let $f(x)= 18 - x^2$ and let $\\mathcal A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(a)+ 2\\left(f(x_1) + f(x_2)+\\ldots + f(x_{n-1})\\right)+f(b) \\right]\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size. \n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[1,4],$ so we have $a=1$ and $b=4.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n} = \\dfrac{4 - 1}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\left[f(1)+ 2\\left(f(2) + f(3)\\right) + f(4) \\right]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $17$ | $14$ | $9$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\left[17 + 2\\left(14 + 9\\right)+ 2 \\right]\\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{65}{2}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[1,4],$ the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area." 13194139535154 true] [17592186091953 1100 "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[1,4.5] = [1,2] \\cup [2,4] \\cup [4,4.5],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |" 13194139535154 true] [17592186091953 1100 "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n$[1,4.5] = [1,2] \\cup [2,4] \\cup [4,4.5]$,\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |" 13194139535154 false] [17592186091953 1145 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/975c49f3fc3bed42149ff267cf17455cd816df4297d6909d0c62072ec5688bd2.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $\\mathcal{A}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/4caf59c8cb226e10b58407abbff7096cac8113bb0193c58aa200dd4c956722e5.png)\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+5}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{5+7}{2}\\cdot (4-2)+\\frac{7+9}{2}\\cdot (4.5-4) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3+12+4 \\\\  & =19\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535154 true] [17592186091953 1145 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/a94ba1a4c5a782a263d6f3afca215ea2.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $\\mathcal{A}$:\n$1$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx \\frac{1 + 5}{2} \\cdot (2 - 1) + \\frac{5 + 7}{2} \\cdot (4 - 2) + \\frac{7 + 9}{2} \\cdot (4.5 - 4); = 3 \\cdot 1 + 6 \\cdot 2 + 8 \\cdot 0.5; = 3 + 12 + 4; = 19\n$$" 13194139535154 false] [17592186091954 1100 "The following table shows the speed of a car, in miles per hour, at certain moments in its $2$-hour journey.\n\n| $t  \\left(\\textrm{hours}\\right)$ | $0$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v  \\left(\\textrm{miles}/\\textrm{hour}\\right)$ | $0$ | $60$ | $40$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,1.5] \\cup [1.5,2],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total distance traveled during the $2$-hour journey." 13194139535154 true] [17592186091954 1100 "The following table shows the speed of a car, in miles per hour, at certain moments in its $2$-hour journey.\n\n| $t \\left(\\textrm{hours}\\right)$ | $0$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v \\left(\\textrm{miles}/\\textrm{hour}\\right)$ | $0$ | $60$ | $40$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n$[0,1.5] \\cup [1.5,2]$,\nto approximate the total distance traveled during the $2$-hour journey." 13194139535154 false] [17592186091954 1145 "In this context, we have $n=2$ trapezoids, and the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ represents the total distance traveled during the $2$-hour journey.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx  \\dfrac{ v(0)+v(1.5) }{2} \\cdot \\Delta t_1 + \\dfrac{ v(1.5)+v(2) }{2} \\cdot \\Delta t_2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{0+60}{2}\\cdot (1.5-0)+\\frac{60+40}{2}\\cdot (2-1.5) \\\\  & =30\\cdot (1.5)+50\\cdot (0.5) \\\\  & =70\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total distance covered during the $2$-hour journey is approximately $70\\,\\textrm{miles}.$" 13194139535154 true] [17592186091954 1145 "In this context, we have $n=2$ trapezoids, and the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ represents the total distance traveled during the $2$-hour journey.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\dfrac{v(0)+v(1.5)}{2} \\cdot \\Delta t_1 + \\dfrac{v(1.5)+v(2)}{2} \\cdot \\Delta t_2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx \\frac{0 + 60}{2} \\cdot (1.5 - 0) + \\frac{60 + 40}{2} \\cdot (2 - 1.5); = 30 \\cdot (1.5) + 50 \\cdot (0.5); = 70\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total distance covered during the $2$-hour journey is approximately $70\\,\\textrm{miles}$." 13194139535154 false] [17592186092077 1100 "For which of the graphs shown below would the trapezoidal sum give an underestimate of the area under the curve between $x=-2$ and $x=2$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/17dbb517c96a210f0b0f96e115c8a808.png)" 13194139535154 false] [17592186092077 1100 "For which of the graphs shown below would the trapezoidal sum give an underestimate of the area under the curve between $x=-2$ and $x=2?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/97f48d1d5c9f54e5351a6f3453fe948959d26a36d30d74f9425bf40e4fd54413.png)" 13194139535154 true] [17592186092077 1145 "The trapezoidal rule underestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave down over the given interval.\n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave down on $[-2,2]$ is the one shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/d74f9ebc9f6cc5895cd84281803d66ce.png)" 13194139535154 false] [17592186092077 1145 "The trapezoidal rule underestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave down over the given interval.     \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave down on $[-2,2]$ is the one shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/2fa5a66712fede3f28f13d5daeaf4b8bc9a7e37eca6e438b16ccffed39514d50.png)" 13194139535154 true] [17592186092126 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334172 13194139535154 true] [17592186092126 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334182 13194139535154 true] [17592186092126 1020 #edb/re