a under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3$ | $7$ |" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334151 1113 #uuid "c429ee66-b024-55a3-ae66-fa1cf23eaccf" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334151 1145 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/8fe84e0056eaa8320b4824740a259226b1a18ff1b64ba648d8740d6cac8703fc.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)+f(5)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+3}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{3+7}{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2+15 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334153 13194139535154 true] [17592186334152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334154 13194139535154 true] [17592186334152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334155 13194139535154 true] [17592186334152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334156 13194139535154 true] [17592186334152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334157 13194139535154 true] [17592186334152 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535154 true] [17592186334152 1041 "selection" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334152 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334157 13194139535154 true] [17592186334152 1110 #uuid "47ec45e6-b931-5f94-b529-28778acf145f" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334153 1034 "28" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334153 1111 #uuid "e0714a1d-dab9-5277-9eda-ced86236f64e" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334154 1034 "34" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334154 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334154 1111 #uuid "57866f5c-40e1-5450-a965-5bcf11b3c496" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334155 1034 "7" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334155 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334155 1111 #uuid "adb03fc7-8074-53dd-8384-7727d54abff1" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334156 1034 "14" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334156 1111 #uuid "bcdf8516-8c3c-5b22-a90a-8a436cc55863" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334157 1034 "17" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334157 1111 #uuid "c806ecea-9b5b-51a2-8ff0-dcdee46b4412" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334158 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334159 13194139535154 true] [17592186334158 1089 "q-116300" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334158 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535154 true] [17592186334158 1100 "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-3,2]=[-3,-1]\\cup [-1,0]\\cup [0,2],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $6$ |" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334158 1113 #uuid "5abc5349-5c55-5963-9a71-982535e3090d" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334158 1145 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/25dc419eb5711b0d990909c611a6eacb2754285fb45dc0d969207e63dc09ba12.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(-1)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-1)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{2+4}{2}\\cdot (-1-(-3))+\\frac{4+8}{2}\\cdot (0-(-1))+\\frac{8+6}{2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+6+14 \\\\  & =26\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334160 13194139535154 true] [17592186334159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334161 13194139535154 true] [17592186334159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334162 13194139535154 true] [17592186334159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334163 13194139535154 true] [17592186334159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334164 13194139535154 true] [17592186334159 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535154 true] [17592186334159 1041 "selection" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334159 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334161 13194139535154 true] [17592186334159 1110 #uuid "d2d18456-cfd7-5bab-b94f-74cb9ea5d189" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334160 1034 "13" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334160 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334160 1111 #uuid "3dfb11b4-1238-5036-a856-f7491620e5a8" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334161 1034 "26" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334161 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334161 1111 #uuid "29885906-9716-5bac-b33b-607ecaaee031" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334162 1034 "34" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334162 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334162 1111 #uuid "389e8326-7153-5ab7-9a60-208f62238518" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334163 1034 "52" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334163 1111 #uuid "f94f567f-26ba-5840-a883-d70712b5221b" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334164 1034 "17" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334164 1111 #uuid "d4d5fc3b-8344-5ae7-ade1-e810c9ecc9de" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334165 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334166 13194139535154 true] [17592186334165 1089 "q-116618" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334165 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535154 true] [17592186334165 1100 "Lisa is selling orange juice. The following table shows the rate, in liters per hour, at which it is sold.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hour})$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $c(t)\\,(\\text{lts/hour})$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[1,2]\\cup [2,5],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total number of liters of juice sold by Lisa over a 4 hours period." 13194139535154 true] [17592186334165 1113 #uuid "ff130200-2cc7-564b-a515-b5f1a6777878" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334165 1145 "In this context, we have $2$ trapezoids, and the area $A$ under the curve $y=c(t)$ represents the total number of liters sold over a $4$ period.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{c(1)+c(2)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{1}+\\frac{c(2)+c(5)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{3+5}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{5+7}{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =22\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Lisa sold approximately $22\\,\\text{liters}$ over a $4$ hours period." 13194139535154 true] [17592186334166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334167 13194139535154 true] [17592186334166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334168 13194139535154 true] [17592186334166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334169 13194139535154 true] [17592186334166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334170 13194139535154 true] [17592186334166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334171 13194139535154 true] [17592186334166 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535154 true] [17592186334166 1041 "selection" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334166 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334169 13194139535154 true] [17592186334166 1110 #uuid "9dd18512-aba0-5661-a3a8-bf18b3dcb0cd" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334167 1034 "$26$ liters" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535154 true] [17592186334167 1111 #uuid "60bd81ef-dc3b-53ff-8610-de655dd68353" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334168 1034 "$18$ liters" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535154 true] [17592186334168 1111 #uuid "c6631b3c-46da-58cf-9101-9c8d243d67f7" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334169 1034 "$22$ liters" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535154 true] [17592186334169 1111 #uuid "32a002ac-3e81-5923-8ccc-f92ad0c26792" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334170 1034 "$32$ liters" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535154 true] [17592186334170 1111 #uuid "84844895-4bf8-5e89-97c7-8acb5f54e244" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334171 1034 "$11$ liters" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535154 true] [17592186334171 1111 #uuid "5a4d52c3-11a4-5fc7-91d6-c823b2ffec79" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334172 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334173 13194139535154 true] [17592186334172 1089 "q-347050" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535154 true] [17592186334172 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/eda6639ced5b182d7a05017883cf15dfbc1ce4b11e4ad3161d0ed6f9d2ab982c.png)\n\nEstimate the area $A$ under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+9x$ over the interval $[0,9]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334172 1113 #uuid "29c3beea-8795-545d-afd1-d68722bc0433" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334172 1145 "Let $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+9x$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,9],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=9.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{9-0}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(0)+2(f(3)+f(6))+f(9)]\\cdot 3.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $18$ | $18$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [0+2(18+18)+0]\\cdot 3=108\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[0,9],$ the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore the approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area." 13194139535154 true] [17592186334173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334174 13194139535154 true] [17592186334173 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535154 true] [17592186334173 1041 "field-1" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334173 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334174 13194139535154 true] [17592186334173 1110 #uuid "12deef7f-43f7-50cf-b65e-fdd89b0b57ac" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334174 1034 "108" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334174 1111 #uuid "08c18582-9114-5864-8e63-ed0a66feeb4e" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334175 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334176 13194139535154 true] [17592186334175 1089 "q-347099" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334175 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535154 true] [17592186334175 1100 "| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $5$ | $6$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,15]=[0,4]\\cup [4,7]\\cup [7,15],\n$$\n\napproximate the area $A$ under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334175 1113 #uuid "8541eab3-b263-590c-8b67-17f74278ebca" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334175 1145 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/a42b281338ce0fc7d64677b260f6c0d8cdaa47855afe35bae0c21fde750a39d5.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(4)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(4)+f(7)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(7)+f(15)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+5}{2}\\cdot (4-0)+\\frac{5+6}{2}\\cdot (7-4)+\\frac{6+2}{2}\\cdot (15-7) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4+\\frac{11}{2}\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =12+\\frac{33}{2}+32 \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334177 13194139535154 true] [17592186334176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334178 13194139535154 true] [17592186334176 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535154 true] [17592186334176 1041 "field-1" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334176 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334178 13194139535154 true] [17592186334176 1110 #uuid "41bb622c-d994-5c6e-ac14-36b7470868d9" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334177 1034 "121" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334177 1111 #uuid "6b862279-c81e-5ce0-9755-09571f2eb2b4" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334178 1034 "\\frac{121}{2}" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334178 1111 #uuid "be9738eb-cd9f-5d58-8e26-0b783ca12442" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334179 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334180 13194139535154 true] [17592186334179 1089 "q-347170" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334179 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535154 true] [17592186334179 1100 "A solar array’s power output rate (in kilowatts) is modeled by $p(t)=-2{t}^{2}+20t.$ Approximate the total number of kilowatt-hours of energy produced in the first nine hours using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\nThe solar array produced  \n{{field-1}}\nkilowatt-hours of energy." 13194139535154 true] [17592186334179 1113 #uuid "017ebb65-acc5-5cb0-bdcd-469277cb48d0" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334179 1145 "In this context, the area $A$ under the curve $y=p(t)$ over the interval $[0,9]$ represents the total number of kilowatt-hours of energy produced in the first nine hours.\n\nThe general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[p(a)+2(p({t}_{1})+p({t}_{2})+…+p({t}_{n-1}))+p(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,9],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=9.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{9-0}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[p(0)+2(p(3)+p(6))+p(9)]\\cdot 3.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $t$ and $p(t):$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(t)$ | $0$ | $42$ | $48$ | $18$ |\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[0+2(42+48)+18]\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[198]\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =297.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solar array produced $297$ kilowatt-hours of energy in the first nine hours." 13194139535154 true] [17592186334180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334181 13194139535154 true] [17592186334180 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535154 true] [17592186334180 1041 "field-1" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334180 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334181 13194139535154 true] [17592186334180 1110 #uuid "4a04e898-02d7-5ce1-9c8e-cd1d485c4b83" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334181 1034 "297" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334181 1111 #uuid "230a3554-140a-5223-9cf7-ae443b7c570e" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334182 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334183 13194139535154 true] [17592186334182 1089 "q-364578" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334182 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535154 true] [17592186334182 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/732c560cfab8b78b2f37ae6623567be2ec2934b34d9b5b40d1c9bebcf0f9e92c.png)\n\nEstimate the area $A$ under the curve $y={x}^{2}+2$ over the interval $[0,6]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334182 1113 #uuid "d0501acb-52f3-5fb0-8ac6-9605e9e2a3bc" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334182 1145 "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,6],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=6.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{6-0}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(0)+2(f(2)+f(4))+f(6)]\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $6$ | $18$ | $38$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [2+2(6+18)+38]\\cdot 2=88\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[0,6],$ the trapezoids lie over the function, and therefore the approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area." 13194139535154 true] [17592186334183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334184 13194139535154 true] [17592186334183 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334185 13194139535154 true] [17592186334183 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535154 true] [17592186334183 1041 "field-1" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334183 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334185 13194139535154 true] [17592186334183 1110 #uuid "e36d7e31-9c5f-514f-90e0-a077cfb1702a" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334184 1034 "84" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334184 1111 #uuid "5ab4f30d-e1a6-548a-84c7-1f52e6196661" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334185 1034 "88" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334185 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334185 1111 #uuid "8fb8588f-6c58-5d5b-a146-abce99257a8d" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334186 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334187 13194139535154 true] [17592186334186 1089 "q-364580" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334186 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535154 true] [17592186334186 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/24fd0ccf835405c3da099e0993a3cebe6711ecd2f0c4c1e960275be3dad032aa.png)\n\nEstimate the area under the curve $y=\\frac{120}{{x}^{2}-1}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $2$ trapezoids of equal height. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334186 1113 #uuid "fbf3c1fd-e8de-5eee-b6f0-4c9d5d50fb48" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334186 1145 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{120}{{x}^{2}-1}$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[2,4],$ so we have $a=2$ and $b=4.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{4-2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(2)+2f(3)+f(4)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $40$ | $15$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [40+2(15)+8]\\cdot 1=39\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[2,4],$ the trapezoids lie over the function, and therefore approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area." 13194139535154 true] [17592186334187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334188 13194139535154 true] [17592186334187 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535154 true] [17592186334187 1041 "field-1" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334187 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334188 13194139535154 true] [17592186334187 1110 #uuid "0f698a13-3cdc-536b-b743-ff3e82edba28" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334188 1034 "39" 13194139535154 true] [17592186334188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535154 true] [17592186334188 1111 #uuid "d680c8a0-cf89-5be7-b9d0-3c294402